<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-creating-your-first-script">
    <title>Creating your first script</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>In this lesson, you will code your first script to make the Godot icon turn in
            circles.
      <term>Scripts attach to a node and extend its behavior</term>. This means that scripts
            inherit all functions and properties of the node they attach to.</p>
    <p>For example, take a game where a Camera2D node follows a ship. The Camera2D node
            follows its parent by default. Imagine you want the camera to shake when the player
            takes damage. As this feature is not built into Godot, you would attach a script
            to the Camera2D node and code the shake.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Shaking a Planet</caption>
        <image source="scripting_camera_shake.gif" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
    </figure>
    <p>This tutorial is written for GDScript.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Tutorial's Desired Outcome</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_rotating_godot.gif" width="50%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <note>
      <title>Seealso</title>
      <p>To learn more about GDScript, its keywords, and its syntax, head to
                the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/index#doc-gdscript">GDScript</url> section. </p>
      </note>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-project-setup">
      <title>Project setup</title>
      <p>Please <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/project_manager#doc-creating-and-importing-projects">create a new project</url> to
                start with a clean slate. Your project should contain one picture: the Godot
                icon, which we often use for prototyping in the community.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Godot Icon</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_icon.svg" width="15%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>We need to create a Sprite2D node to display it in the game. In the <c>Scene</c> dock,
                click the <c>Other Node</c> button.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Other Node Button</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_click_other_node.webp" width="25%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Type "Sprite2D" in the search bar to filter nodes and double-click on Sprite2D
                to create the node.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>New Node Dialog: Sprite2D</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_add_sprite_node.webp" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Your <c>Scene</c> tab should now only have a Sprite2D node.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Scene Tree (One Node)</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_scene_tree.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>A Sprite2D node needs a texture to display. In the <c>Inspector</c> on the right, you
                can see that the <c>Texture</c> property says <c>&lt;empty&gt;</c>. To display the
                Godot icon, click and drag the file <c>icon.svg</c> from the FileSystem dock onto the
                Texture slot.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Inspector: Setting Texture to Icon</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_setting_texture.webp" width="30%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>You can create Sprite2D nodes automatically by dragging and dropping images
                    on the viewport.</p>
        </note>
      <p>Then, click and drag the icon in the viewport to center it in the game view.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Centering The Sprite with Your Mouse</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_centering_sprite.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-creating-a-new-script">
      <title>Creating a new script</title>
      <p>To create and attach a new script to our node, right-click on Sprite2D in the
                Scene dock and select <c>Attach Script</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Attaching Script to Node</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_attach_script.webp" width="50%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The <c>Attach Node Script</c> window appears. It allows you to select the script's
                language and file path, among other options.</p>
      <p>Change the <c>Template</c> field from <c>Node: Default</c> to <c>Object: Empty</c> to
                start with a clean file. Leave the other options set to their default values and
                click the <c>Create</c> button to create the script.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Object Script Template</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_attach_node_script.webp" width="55%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>

      <p>The <c>Script</c> workspace should appear with your new <c>sprite_2d.gd</c> file open and
                the following line of code:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>extends Sprite2D</code>
          </program>


      <p>Every GDScript file is implicitly a class. The <c>extends</c> keyword defines the
                class this script inherits or extends. In this case, it's <c>Sprite2D</c>, meaning
                our script will get access to all the properties and functions of the Sprite2D
                node, including classes it extends, like <c>Node2D</c>, <c>CanvasItem</c>, and
                <c>Node</c>.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>In GDScript, if you omit the line with the <c>extends</c> keyword, your
                    class will implicitly extend <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_refcounted.html#class-refcounted">RefCounted</url>, which
                    Godot uses to manage your application's memory.</p>
        </note>
      <p>Inherited properties include the ones you can see in the <c>Inspector</c> dock, like
                our node's <c>texture</c>.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>By default, the <c>Inspector</c> displays a node's properties in "Title Case", with
                    capitalized words separated by a space. In GDScript code, these properties
                    are in "snake_case", which is lowercase with each word separated by an underscore.</p>
        <p>You can hover over any property's name in the <c>Inspector</c> to see a description and
                    its identifier in code.</p>
        </note>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-hello-world">
      <title>Hello, world!</title>
      <p>Our script currently doesn't do anything. Let's make it print the text "Hello,
                world!" to the Output bottom panel to get started.</p>
      <p>Add the following code to your script:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func _init() -> void:
    print("Hello, world!")</code>
          </program>


      <p>Let's break it down. The <c>func</c> keyword defines a new function named
                <c>_init</c>. This is a special name for our class's constructor. The engine calls
                <c>_init()</c> on every object or node upon creating it in memory, if you define
                this function.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>GDScript is an indent-based language. The tab at the start of the line
                    that says <c>print()</c> is necessary for the code to work. If you omit
                    it or don't indent a line correctly, the editor will highlight it in
                    red and display the following error message: "Indented block expected".</p>
        </note>


        <activity xml:id="first-script-hello-world" label="first-script-hello-world">
            <title>Simple Print</title>
            <statement><p>Print Hello, world! here.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-hello-world" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-hello-world"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P3.sprite2d_hello_world"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P3.sprite2d_hello_world/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D

func _init() -> void:
    pass

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"

func _build_checks() -> void:

   #
   var check_pattern := _add_simple_check(
      "Program should print 'Hello, world!",
      func() -> String:
         return (
            ""
            if _is_code_line_match(["print(?Hello,world!?)","print(?Hello,World!?)"])
            else "couldn't find a print('Hello, world!') call "
         )
   )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>

      <p>Save the scene as <c>sprite_2d.tscn</c> if you haven't already, then press <c>F6</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>R</c> on macOS)
                to run it. Look at the <c>Output</c> bottom panel that expands.
                It should display "Hello, world!".</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Output Bottom Panel</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_print_hello_world.webp" width="40%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Delete the <c>_init()</c> function, so you're only left with the line <c>extends
Sprite2D</c>.</p>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-turning-around">
      <title>Turning around</title>
  <introduction>
      <p>It's time to make our node move and rotate. To do so, we're going to add two
                member variables to our script: the movement speed in pixels per second and the
                angular speed in radians per second. Add the following after the <c>extends Sprite2D</c> line.</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>var speed: float = 400
var angular_speed: float = PI</code>
          </program>


      <p>Member variables sit near the top of the script, after any "extends" lines,
                but before functions. Every node
                instance with this script attached to it will have its own copy of the <c>speed</c>
                and <c>angular_speed</c> properties.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Angles in Godot work in radians by default,
                    but you have built-in functions and properties available if you prefer
                    to calculate angles in degrees instead.</p>
        </note>
      <p>To move our icon, we need to update its position and rotation every frame in the
                game loop. We can use the <c>_process()</c> virtual function of the <c>Node</c> class.
                If you define it in any class that extends the Node class, like Sprite2D, Godot
                will call the function every frame and pass it an argument named <c>delta</c>, the
                time elapsed since the last frame.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Games work by rendering many images per second, each called a frame, and
                    they do so in a loop. We measure the rate at which a game produces images in
                    Frames Per Second (FPS). Most games aim for 60 FPS, although you might find
                    figures like 30 FPS on slower mobile devices or 90 to 240 for virtual
                    reality games.</p>
        <p>The engine and game developers do their best to update the game world and
                    render images at a constant time interval, but there are always small
                    variations in frame render times. That's why the engine provides us with
                    this delta time value, making our motion independent of our framerate.</p>
        </note>
      <p>At the bottom of the script, define the function:</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    rotation += angular_speed * delta</code>
          </program>


      <p>The <c>func</c> keyword defines a new function. After it, we have to write the
                function's name and arguments it takes in parentheses. A colon ends the
                definition, and the indented blocks that follow are the function's content or
                instructions.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Notice how <c>_process()</c>, like <c>_init()</c>, starts with a leading
                    underscore. By convention, Godot's virtual functions, that is to say,
                    built-in functions you can override to communicate with the engine,
                    start with an underscore.</p>
        </note>
      <p>The line inside the function, <c>rotation += angular_speed * delta</c>, increments
                our sprite's rotation every frame. Here, <c>rotation</c> is a property inherited
                from the class <c>Node2D</c>, which <c>Sprite2D</c> extends. It controls the rotation
                of our node and works with radians.</p>
      <note>
        <title>Tip</title>
        <p>In the code editor, you can <c>Ctrl</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>Click</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
          <c />+<c />
          <c>Click</c> on
                    macOS) on any built-in property or function like <c>position</c>,
                    <c>rotation</c>, or <c>_process</c> to open the corresponding documentation
                    in a new tab.</p>
        </note>
      <p>Run the scene to see the Godot icon turn in-place.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Icon Turning In Place</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_godot_turning_in_place.gif" width="50%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
     <activity xml:id="first-script-rotate" label="first-script-rotate">
            <title>Rotate</title>
            <statement><p>Add Rotation code here.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-sprite2d-rotate" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-sprite2d-rotate"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P4.sprite2d_rotate"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P4.sprite2d_rotate/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D

var angular_speed: float = PI

func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    pass

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"

# L1.P4 - rotate comparison
#
# A "graphical" GDPractice example: motion, compared frame by
# frame between practice and solution via _test_space. The SplitLayout shows
# both the student's icon and the solution's icon rotatating
# the same icon simultaneously.
#

func _init() -> void:
  side_by_side = true


# How long to sample for, in seconds.
const SAMPLE_DURATION := 1.0



func _build_requirements() -> void:
  super()
  _add_properties_requirement(["angular_speed"])


# Reset both nodes to Vector2.ZERO and mirror the practice's speed onto the
# solution before sampling begins.
func _setup_state() -> void:
  _practice.position = _solution.position
  _practice.rotation = _solution.rotation
  _solution.angular_speed = _practice.angular_speed


# Simulate move_right being held for SAMPLE_DURATION seconds while sampling.
func _setup_populate_test_space() -> void:
  await _connect_timed(SAMPLE_DURATION, get_tree().physics_frame, _sample_test_space)


func _sample_test_space() -> void:
  _test_space.append({
    practice_rotation = _practice.rotation,
    solution_rotation = _solution.rotation,
    delta = get_physics_process_delta_time(),
  })


func _build_checks() -> void:


  _add_simple_check(
    "Rotation takes delta into account",
    func() -> String:
      var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
        var displacement: float = y.practice_rotation - x.practice_rotation
        var expected: float = y.solution_rotation - x.solution_rotation
        return is_equal_approx(displacement,expected)
      if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
        return (
          "Each frame's rotation should match the solution's. "
          + "Make sure you multiply angular_speed by delta."
        )
      return ""
  )

  _add_simple_check(
    "Rotation matches the solution every frame",
    func() -> String:
      var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
        return is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation)
      if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
        for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
          if not is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation):
            return (
              "practice rotation %s doesn't match solution rotation %s."
              % [sample.practice_rotation, sample.solution_rotation]
            )
      return ""
  )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>
</introduction>
      <subsubsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-moving-forward">
        <title>Moving forward</title>
        <p>Let's now make the node move. Add the following two lines inside of the <c>_process()</c>
                    function, ensuring the new lines are indented the same way as the <c>rotation += angular_speed * delta</c> line before
                    them.</p>

          <program language="gdscript">
            <code>var velocity: Vector2 = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed

position += velocity * delta</code>
            </program>

             <activity xml:id="first-script-move" label="first-script-move">
            <title>Move</title>
            <statement><p>Add Rotation and move code here.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-sprite2d-move" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-sprite2d-move"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P5.sprite2d_move"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P5.sprite2d_move/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D
var speed: float = 400
var angular_speed: float = PI

func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    pass

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"

# L1.P5 - move comparison
#
# A "graphical" GDPractice example: motion, compared frame by
# frame between practice and solution via _test_space. The SplitLayout shows
# both the student's icon and the solution's icon rotatating
# the same icon simultaneously.
#

func _init() -> void:
  side_by_side = true


# How long to sample for, in seconds.
const SAMPLE_DURATION := 1.0



func _build_requirements() -> void:
  super()
  _add_properties_requirement(["angular_speed"])
  _add_properties_requirement(["speed"])


# Reset both nodes to Vector2.ZERO and mirror the practice's speed onto the
# solution before sampling begins.
func _setup_state() -> void:
  _practice.position = _solution.position
  _practice.rotation = _solution.rotation
  _solution.angular_speed = _practice.angular_speed
  _solution.speed = _practice.speed

# Simulate move_right being held for SAMPLE_DURATION seconds while sampling.
func _setup_populate_test_space() -> void:
  await _connect_timed(SAMPLE_DURATION, get_tree().physics_frame, _sample_test_space)


func _sample_test_space() -> void:
  _test_space.append({
    practice_rotation = _practice.rotation,
    solution_rotation = _solution.rotation,
    practice_position = _practice.position,
    solution_position = _solution.position,
    delta = get_physics_process_delta_time(),
  })


func _build_checks() -> void:


  _add_simple_check(
    "Rotation takes delta into account",
    func() -> String:
      var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
        var displacement: float = y.practice_rotation - x.practice_rotation
        var expected: float = y.solution_rotation - x.solution_rotation
        return is_equal_approx(displacement,expected)
      if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
        return (
          "Each frame's rotation should match the solution's. "
          + "Make sure you multiply angular_speed by delta."
        )
      return ""
  )

  _add_simple_check(
    "Rotation matches the solution every frame",
    func() -> String:
      var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
        return is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation)
      if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
        for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
          if not is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation):
            return (
              "practice rotation %s doesn't match solution rotation %s."
              % [sample.practice_rotation, sample.solution_rotation]
            )
      return ""
  )

  _add_simple_check(
    "Movement takes delta into account",
    func() -> String:
      var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
        var displacement: Vector2 = y.practice_position - x.practice_position
        var expected: Vector2 = y.solution_position - x.solution_position
        return displacement.is_equal_approx(expected)
      if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
        return (
          "Each frame's displacement should match the solution's. "
          + "Make sure you multiply velocity by delta."
        )
      return ""
  )

  _add_simple_check(
    "Position matches the solution every frame",
    func() -> String:
      var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
        return sample.practice_position.is_equal_approx(sample.solution_position)
      if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
        for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
          if not sample.practice_position.is_equal_approx(sample.solution_position):
            return (
              "practice position %s doesn't match solution position %s."
              % [sample.practice_position, sample.solution_position]
            )
      return ""
  )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>
        <p>As we already saw, the <c>var</c> keyword defines a new variable. If you put it at
                    the top of the script, it defines a property of the class. Inside a function, it
                    defines a local variable: it only exists within the function's scope.</p>
        <p>We define a local variable named <c>velocity</c>, a 2D vector representing both a
                    direction and a speed. To make the node move forward, we start from the Vector2
                    class's constant <c>Vector2.UP</c>, a vector pointing up, and rotate it by calling the
                    Vector2 method <c>rotated()</c>. This expression, <c>Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation)</c>,
                    is a vector pointing forward relative to our icon. Multiplied by our <c>speed</c>
                    property, it gives us a velocity we can use to move the node forward.</p>
        <p>We add <c>velocity * delta</c> to the node's <c>position</c> to move it. The position
                    itself is of type <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_vector2.html#class-vector2">Vector2</url>, a built-in type in Godot
                    representing a 2D vector.</p>
        <p>Run the scene to see the Godot head run in circles.</p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Rotate and Move</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_rotating_godot.gif" width="50%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
          </figure>
        <note>
          <title>Note</title>
          <p>Moving a node like that does not take into account colliding with
                        walls or the floor. In <xref ref="getting-started-first-2d-game-index-your-first-2d-game" text="custom">Your first 2D game</xref>, you will learn
                        another approach to moving objects while detecting collisions.</p>
          </note>
        <p>Our node currently moves by itself. In the next part,
                    <xref ref="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-listening-to-player-input" text="custom">Listening to player input</xref>, we'll use player input to control it.</p>
        </subsubsection>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-complete-script">
      <title>Complete script</title>
      <p>Here is the complete <c>sprite_2d.gd</c> file for reference.</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>extends Sprite2D

var speed: float = 400
var angular_speed: float = PI


func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    rotation += angular_speed * delta

    var velocity: Vector2 = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed

    position += velocity * delta</code>
          </program>


      </subsection>
    </section>
